<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nine Years' War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1688">1688</a>–<a href="/years/1697">1697</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as War of the Grand Alliance, War of the League of Augsburg · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nine Years' War was fought from 1688 to 1697 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the Grand Alliance led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-england">William III of England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Savoy. French armies devastated the Palatinate and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, burning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, Speyer, Worms and many smaller towns in 1689. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ryswick">Treaty of Ryswick</a> in 1697 ended the war with modest French gains.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nine-years-war.jpg" alt="Nine Years' War" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DavidDijkgraaf · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collage_Nine_Years%27_War.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> of 1678 ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Franco-Dutch War</a> at the high point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s expansion. Over the following years he used courts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besancon">Besançon</a> and Breisach, which usually found in his favour, to claim territory under the vague wording of earlier treaties, and by 1682 he had secured the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> except Strasbourg. The Truce of Ratisbon of 1684 guaranteed these borders for twenty years. After 1685 his revocation of the Edict of Nantes sent as many as 200,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> into exile and helped William of Orange build his long-sought alliance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In September 1688 Louis led an army across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> to press the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> into accepting his claims. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippsburg">Philippsburg</a> fell on 30 October and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a> capitulated on 11 November. That month the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s resources to William. James II, sheltered by Louis, landed in Ireland on 12 March 1689 with 6,000 French troops, but his forces could not take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Derry</a>. On 12 May 1689 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and the Holy Roman Emperor Leopold I signed an offensive compact in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, the core of the Grand Alliance.</p><p>The main fighting took place around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s borders: in the Spanish Netherlands, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, Savoy and Catalonia. Marshal Luxembourg defeated the Prince of Waldeck at the Battle of Fleurus on 1 July 1690, but the victory brought little benefit. The war was dominated by sieges and the defence of fortresses, and by 1695 field armies had grown to nearly 100,000 men, which drove the Maritime Powers into a fiscal crisis while France struggled under a shattered economy. The conflict spread overseas as King William's War in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, and in 1693 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took Pondichéry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Secret talks began in 1695, and by 1696 the main belligerents were financially exhausted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The peace conference opened in May 1697 at William III's palace at Ryswick near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, and the settlement was largely worked out through private talks between Marshal Boufflers and William Bentinck, the Earl of Portland. France kept the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> but gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and its gains on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, which were restored to their rulers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> recognised William III as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> gained barrier fortresses in the Spanish Netherlands and a favourable commercial treaty, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> regained Luxembourg and other territories.</p><p>Both sides saw the peace as a pause, since it did not settle who would succeed the childless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II of Spain</a>, a question that had dominated European politics for over thirty years. It led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> in 1701.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nine Years' War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Palatinate suffered again in the <b>Nine Years' War</b>, when Louis XIV's troops, pressing the inheritance claims of Elizabeth Charlotte, duchess of Orléans, systematically burned Heidelberg, Mannheim, Speyer and Worms in 1689, and Heidelberg Castle was blown up in 1689 and 1693.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The song grew out of the long enmity between France and Germany over the left bank of the Rhine, which French armies had occupied in the <b>Nine Years' War</b> and again in the Napoleonic Wars.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-wacht-am-rhein">Die Wacht am Rhein</a></li><li><blockquote>French troops burned it in 1689 during the <b>Nine Years' War</b>, and it was rebuilt in Baroque style.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-gengenbach">Free Imperial City of Gengenbach</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1689 French troops burned Speyer during the <b>Nine Years' War</b>, and the court moved to Wetzlar.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-speyer">Free Imperial City of Speyer</a></li></ul></section>
